# When did each run happen?
#
# The run -> instant map, on its own, because joining it to the readings inside
# one query costs a hundred times what the two cost apart. Measured against the
# deployed store on 2026-09-18, 251,798 quads across 121 run graphs:
#
#   this query alone                          0.00s    121 rows
#   endpoint_readings.rq alone                0.02s  1,107 rows
#   the two as one query, joined on ?run      2.25s  1,107 rows
#
# The one query was `endpoint_history.rq`, and it made the endpoint page take
# 2.5 seconds. It matched `?activity a prov:Activity ; prov:generatedAtTime
# ?generatedAt` inside `GRAPH ?run` beside a UNION of the readings, and the
# planner evidently re-derives the activity match per reading rather than
# building this 121-row map once. The join is a dict lookup in Python and the
# page is the only caller, so nothing is lost by doing it there.
#
# IT GETS WORSE WITH TIME, which is why this was worth splitting rather than
# tolerating: the cost scales with the number of run graphs and the sweep adds
# twenty-four a day.
#
# ONE ROW PER RUN. An activity is written once per sweep, so this needs no
# DISTINCT; a run graph with no activity has no instant and is correctly absent
# rather than sorted as if it had one.

PREFIX prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#>

SELECT ?run ?generatedAt
WHERE {
  GRAPH ?run {
    ?activity a prov:Activity ;
              prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
  }
}
